  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 241 AM EDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nWednesday - Thursday Night...\n\nThe hottest day of the week looks to be Wednesday as upper ridging \ncontinues to dominate the southern half of the CONUS. Meanwhile, a \nsteady SW surface flow will set up over our region between high \npressure centered off the mid Atlantic coast, and a strong surface \nlow over eastern Canada. In fact, the pressure gradient will be \nquite impressive for this time of year, and looks like we could see \nsome 20-25 mph gusts from a very well mixed boundary layer. This \nlooks like a pattern where temps could overachieve, so like where \nthe NBM is toward the higher side of the guidance envelope, and \nsimilar to the 90th percentile ECM temp guidance. Peak heat indices \ncould be a bit of a concern for Wednesday as they will likely reach \ninto the low 100s for a time. Expect afternoon mixing may lower dew \npoints enough through the afternoon to keep any Heat Advisory \nheadlines from being needed, but it will be close. Overall, low to \nmid 90s expected for highs, with shower and storm chances holding \noff until later Wednesday night into Thursday.\n\nA cold front trailing from the aforementioned strong eastern Canada \nsurface low will approach for Thursday. Ahead of this feature, \nfairly widespread coverage of showers and storms is anticipated to \ndrop into and through our region. Looks to be a fairly strong amount \nof deep layer shear across the northern half of our CWA where 35-45 \nknots may be realized. Across southern KY, values would be weaker \nand more in the 25-35 knot range. Weak to modest instability and \nparticularly weak mid level lapse rates could be the limiting factor \nfor whether any strong to severe storms will be possible, and timing \nof the frontal passage on Thursday will have a lot to do with that. \nShould fairly strong storms develop, then we may have to watch for a \nbit of training as the deep layer flow will be nearly parallel to \nthe surface boundary. A few localized areas could see some higher \nrain totals in this setup, but overall the expected widespread \nrainfall between .25\" and .5\" for most will be welcome. Right now, \nLREF probabilities of 1\" or more drop off to 10-15% or less. \n\nFriday - Sunday...\n\nThings are expected to be a bit cooler and overall drier by Friday \nas high pressure builds in behind the cold front. Perhaps a few \nshowers linger across our southern CWA, but will only keep low \nchances in here. Temps settle back into the mid and upper 80s to end \nthe week, at least briefly. The overall dry pattern should continue \ninto the weekend although some weak troughing may allow for a few \nnon-zero shower or storm chances. Better chances for the upper ridge \nto build back in by late weekend, and this will allow temps to rise \nback into the upper 80s and low 90s if it occurs. Only low to medium \nconfidence in the weekend for now given some uncertainty in how the \nupper pattern will evolve by this time.",
